1) List

A list stores elements in the order they are inserted.

> List maintains insertion order
> Allows duplicate elements
> Allows null values
> Allows accessing elements using index

    a) ArrayList: Uses a dynamic array internally
                  Fast for retrieving elements
                  Slower for insertion and deletion in the middle
                  Uses less memory compared to Linked List

                  Use ArrayList when:
                  We frequently read data.
                  Insertions/deletions are less frequent.

                  Examples:
                  E-Commerce Shopping Carts - Holds items as a user clicks "Add to Cart".
                  Expands in size when more products are added and shrinks if an item is deleted

                  Music and Video Playlists - Manages streaming queues on apps like Spotify or Netflix.
                  Allows songs or movies to be inserted, deleted, or reordered dynamically.

                  Task & To Do Lists
                  Keeps track of daily chores or work items where tasks are constantly checked off (removed) or added

    b) LinkedList : Uses Doubly Linked List internally
                    Faster for insertion/deletion operations
                    Slower for read operations or random access
                    Uses more memory as it has to store both references (previous & next element)

                    Use Linked List when frequent insertion or deletion operations are required

                    Example: In the real world, linked lists are used when you need to dynamically
                    add or remove items without resizing a fixed container. Java provides a built-in
                    implementation via the java.util.LinkedList class, which operates as a doubly linked list.

                    Music Player PlayList
                    GPS Navigation Routes
                    Undo/Redo Layers

    c) Stack: Follows LIFO - Last In First Out

              The last element to be added in the stack is the first one ro be removed from the stack
              Main operations - push(), pop(), peak()

              push() - to add an element in to the stack
              pop()  - to remove an element from the stack
              peak() - returns the next element that will be removed from the stack

    d) Vector: Legacy data structure


